{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DZ1Vx2GohNpBwMMuKJn6Bs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Loop Hook Dispatch Contract\n2\t\n3\tGeneric reference for consuming the `--raw` JSON output of `gsd_run loop render-hooks <point>`\n4\tin any host-loop workflow. This document is point-agnostic — it applies to every loop\n5\textension point (discuss:pre, discuss:post, plan:pre, plan:post, execute:pre, execute:wave:pre,\n6\texecute:wave:post, execute:post, verify:pre, verify:post, ship:pre, ship:post).\n7\t\n8\t## Envelope shape\n9\t\n10\t```json\n11\t{\n12\t  \"point\": \"discuss:pre\",\n13\t  \"activeHooks\": [\n14\t    { \"kind\": \"contribution\", \"into\": \"orchestrator\", \"fragment\": { \"inline\": \"...\" } },\n15\t    { \"kind\": \"step\", \"ref\": { \"skill\": \"my-skill\" } },\n16\t    { \"kind\": \"gate\", \"check\": { \"query\": \"...\" }, \"blocking\": true, \"onError\": \"skip\" }\n17\t  ],\n18\t  \"rendered\": \"...\"\n19\t}\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\t`activeHooks` is an array of enabled hook entries for the named point. It is empty (or absent)\n23\twhen no capability has registered an active hook at this point — treat that as a no-op.\n24\t\n25\t## Dispatch rules by `kind`\n26\t\n27\t### `contribution`\n28\t\n29\tInject `fragment.inline` verbatim into the context for the role named in `into`\n30\t(e.g. `orchestrator`, `planner`). Do not paraphrase — the text is the product.\n31\t\n32\t### `step`\n33\t\n34\tDispatch the referenced unit. Exactly one of `ref.skill`, `ref.agent`, or `ref.command` is set.\n35\t\n36\t- `ref.skill` present → dispatch via the Skill tool with skill id `gsd-<ref.skill>`.\n37\t- `ref.agent` present → dispatch via the Agent tool with `subagent_type` = `ref.agent`.\n38\t  Before dispatching an agent, print the canonical liveness banner so users know silence\n39\t  is expected and do not kill a healthy agent:\n40\t\n41\t  ```\n42\t  ◆ Spawning <agent>... (runs in a subagent — no output until it returns; expected, not a freeze)\n43\t  ```\n44\t\n45\t- `ref.command` present → **validate it IN-CONTEXT first, before any shell use.** It comes\n46\t  from a capability manifest, which may be third-party. Check the value you read from\n47\t  `activeHooks` against `^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*( [a-z][a-z0-9-]*)*$` yourself — **never** by pasting\n48\t  it into a shell command to be tested there, because a value carrying a quote, `;`,\n49\t  `` ` ``, `$(`, or a newline would terminate the assignment and run as its own statement\n50\t  before any shell-side check could execute. A value that fails is a malformed manifest:\n51\t  record a warning, skip that hook, continue to the next entry. Only a value that has passed\n52\t  is run, with the phase number appended:\n53\t\n54\t  ```bash\n55\t  gsd_run ${ref.command} --phase \"${PHASE_NUMBER}\" --raw\n56\t  ```\n57\t\n58\tWait for the result before continuing to the next hook or the next step.\n59\t\n60\t**`supportsReviewerLanes` (optional, boolean).** A `step` entry may carry\n61\t`supportsReviewerLanes: true` alongside `ref` (#4209). A workflow opts a step into external\n62\treviewer-lane dispatch by calling `gsd_run review-lane dispatch-step --cap-id <capId> --point\n63\t<point> --explicit <slugs> ...` — `dispatch-step` resolves its OWN active hook for `<point>` (via\n64\t`resolveActiveHooksForPoint`, the same in-process resolver `loop render-hooks` itself calls) and\n65\tchecks whether `<capId>`'s hook carries this field before proceeding; the workflow does not\n66\tresolve or gate on the trait itself, only passes the two flags naming which step it is. When the\n67\ttrait reads exactly `true`, `dispatch-step` routes through `dispatchReviewerLanes`, the one\n68\tinterpreter in `src/reviewer-step-dispatch.cts` that reuses the existing reviewer-lane selection,\n69\tplanning, and invocation machinery, so any explicitly selected reviewer lane also reviews the\n70\tsame scope. Absent or `false` is inert: `dispatch-step` itself is a no-op (a non-boolean value is\n71\trejected by `capability-validator.cjs` at load time, so it never reaches `dispatch-step` at all).\n72\tThis is the only place a step opts into reviewer-lane support: a capability beyond `code-review`\n73\treuses it by declaring the same trait on its own step and calling `dispatch-step` with\n74\t`--cap-id`/`--point`, with zero bespoke TRAIT-RESOLUTION code of its own. The workflow still owns\n75\tmatching its own CLI flags against the reviewer-lane roster and assembling the evidence block\n76\thanded to its consolidator — those are NOT part of what this trait makes reusable.\n77\t\n78\tA `step` is **advisory by construction**: it never blocks or redirects the host workflow —\n79\tthat is what a `gate` is for. Each dispatch is best-effort; on error record a warning and\n80\tcontinue, honoring `onError`.\n81\t\n82\t**A point whose workflow hand-rolls one `kind` does not implement this contract.** Several\n83\thost workflows historically matched a single hook (e.g. `execute:post` matched only\n84\t`ref.skill == \"code-review\"`), so any other step registered there was declared and silently\n85\tnever run. When a workflow defers to this file, it dispatches **every** active `step` entry,\n86\tnot one shape of one.\n87\t\n88\t### `gate`\n89\t\n90\t**Validate `check` before any shell use.** `check.query` and `check.predicate` come from a\n91\tcapability manifest, which may be third-party — and `gates[].check` is **not** one of the\n92\texecutable surfaces the install consent prompt discloses (`hooks`, command modules,\n93\t`mcpServers`, reviewer lanes), so a capability can be consented to as declarative-only and\n94\tstill reach a shell through a gate. Normalize first, then validate: the registry declares query\n95\tids in dotted form (`ui.plan-gate`, `verify.codebase-drift`) and `gsd_run check` accepts them by\n96\tturning each `.` into `-`, so replace every `.` with `-` in the query value you read from\n97\t`activeHooks`, then check that normalized value against\n98\t`^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*( [a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*)*$` yourself, IN-CONTEXT — **never** by\n99\tpasting it into a shell command to be tested there, because a value carrying a quote, `;`, a\n100\t`` ` ``, `$(`, or a newline would terminate the assignment and run as its own statement before\n101\tany shell-side check could execute. The normalized value is the one value you validate and the\n102\tone value you pass: wherever a workflow shows `${hook.check.query}`, write the normalized value.\n103\tA leading dot, a trailing dot or consecutive dots become a leading, trailing or doubled hyphen,\n104\twhich the pattern rejects. A value that fails — or that passes but names no known check, which\n105\t`gsd_run check` rejects as an unknown check subcommand — is a malformed manifest: record a\n106\twarning, route it per the gate's `onError`, and do not run it. Pass `check.predicate` as a **single argv\n107\telement** for the same reason — never re-quote it into a shell string, where an apostrophe\n108\twould close the literal. This is the identical requirement `step` → `ref.command` carries\n109\tabove; it was stated there and omitted here, which is the gap #3559 closed.\n110\t\n111\tEvaluate `check` (one of `query`, `predicate`, or `agentVerdict`). Then honor `blocking`:\n112\t\n113\t- `blocking: true` → if the check returns `block: true`, surface `check.message` to the user\n114\t  and stop the current step. Do not continue.\n115\t- `blocking: false` → advisory only; surface the message but continue regardless of outcome.\n116\t\n117\tHonor `onError` if the check itself errors: `skip` means treat as non-blocking and continue;\n118\t`halt` means surface the error and stop. \"Errors\" means a non-zero exit from `gsd_run check ...`,\n119\tand that includes `69` (`UNAVAILABLE`, #5170): a gate verb exits `69` when it **could not look** at\n120\tits evidence, still printing the gate's usual JSON (its own fields name what could not be read, such\n121\tas `status: \"unresolvable\"`, `readError`, or `unreadable`). That JSON is not a verdict — do not act\n122\ton its `block`; route it by `onError` as a step-1 command failure. Exit `0` always means a\n123\tdelivered verdict, a blocking one included (`block: true`): a gate verb never exits non-zero to say\n124\t\"blocked\".\n125\t\n126\t## Empty / absent `activeHooks`\n127\t\n128\tIf `activeHooks` is absent, null, or an empty array, skip silently and continue to the next\n129\tstep in the workflow. No output to the user is needed.\n130\t\n131\t## The `execute:task` point (a different shape)\n132\t\n133\t`execute:task` exists below wave granularity — it is evaluated once per task, inside the\n134\t`execute:wave:pre` / `execute:wave:post` bracket, immediately before that task's `read_first`\n135\tgate. It is **not** one of the 12 points documented above, does not appear in `steps` /\n136\t`contributions` / `gates`, and is never dispatched through `gsd_run loop render-hooks <point>` or\n137\tthis file's `activeHooks` envelope.\n138\t\n139\tInstead, a capability declares task-content resolution directly in its manifest body via\n140\t`taskContentResolver` (`trackerPrefix` + a bounded `invoke`) — see\n141\t[Capability manifest reference](../../docs/reference/capability-manifest.md). `execute-plan.md`'s\n142\tper-task loop calls `gsd_run task resolve-content --plan <path> --task-id <tracker-id> --raw`\n143\tdirectly, an unconditional, required subprocess invocation with a real, binding exit code —\n144\tnever a prose-dispatched `step`/`gate` entry chosen from an `activeHooks` array.\n145\t\n146\tThis point always runs — there is no `when` config gate and no autonomous-mode elision. That is\n147\tdeliberate, not an oversight: the twelve points above are best-effort prose dispatch, which\n148\t`execute:task`'s hard-halt safety property cannot be built on top of (a missed dispatch is\n149\tindistinguishable from a legitimate resolver-empty fallback). See\n150\t[ADR-3646](../../docs/adr/3646-per-task-content-resolution-seam.md) for the full rationale,\n151\tincluding why a `kind: \"gate\"` shape was rejected outright.\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"uuid-7","uuid":"uuid-330","timestamp":"2026-11-02T18:54:34.666Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"~/.claude/gsd-core/references/loop-hook-dispatch.md","content":"# Loop Hook Dispatch Contract\n\nGeneric reference for consuming the `--raw` JSON output of `gsd_run loop render-hooks <point>`\nin any host-loop workflow. This document is point-agnostic — it applies to every loop\nextension point (discuss:pre, discuss:post, plan:pre, plan:post, execute:pre, execute:wave:pre,\nexecute:wave:post, execute:post, verify:pre, verify:post, ship:pre, ship:post).\n\n## Envelope shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"point\": \"discuss:pre\",\n  \"activeHooks\": [\n    { \"kind\": \"contribution\", \"into\": \"orchestrator\", \"fragment\": { \"inline\": \"...\" } },\n    { \"kind\": \"step\", \"ref\": { \"skill\": \"my-skill\" } },\n    { \"kind\": \"gate\", \"check\": { \"query\": \"...\" }, \"blocking\": true, \"onError\": \"skip\" }\n  ],\n  \"rendered\": \"...\"\n}\n```\n\n`activeHooks` is an array of enabled hook entries for the named point. It is empty (or absent)\nwhen no capability has registered an active hook at this point — treat that as a no-op.\n\n## Dispatch rules by `kind`\n\n### `contribution`\n\nInject `fragment.inline` verbatim into the context for the role named in `into`\n(e.g. `orchestrator`, `planner`). Do not paraphrase — the text is the product.\n\n### `step`\n\nDispatch the referenced unit. Exactly one of `ref.skill`, `ref.agent`, or `ref.command` is set.\n\n- `ref.skill` present → dispatch via the Skill tool with skill id `gsd-<ref.skill>`.\n- `ref.agent` present → dispatch via the Agent tool with `subagent_type` = `ref.agent`.\n  Before dispatching an agent, print the canonical liveness banner so users know silence\n  is expected and do not kill a healthy agent:\n\n  ```\n  ◆ Spawning <agent>... (runs in a subagent — no output until it returns; expected, not a freeze)\n  ```\n\n- `ref.command` present → **validate it IN-CONTEXT first, before any shell use.** It comes\n  from a capability manifest, which may be third-party. Check the value you read from\n  `activeHooks` against `^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*( [a-z][a-z0-9-]*)*$` yourself — **never** by pasting\n  it into a shell command to be tested there, because a value carrying a quote, `;`,\n  `` ` ``, `$(`, or a newline would terminate the assignment and run as its own statement\n  before any shell-side check could execute. A value that fails is a malformed manifest:\n  record a warning, skip that hook, continue to the next entry. Only a value that has passed\n  is run, with the phase number appended:\n\n  ```bash\n  gsd_run ${ref.command} --phase \"${PHASE_NUMBER}\" --raw\n  ```\n\nWait for the result before continuing to the next hook or the next step.\n\n**`supportsReviewerLanes` (optional, boolean).** A `step` entry may carry\n`supportsReviewerLanes: true` alongside `ref` (#4209). A workflow opts a step into external\nreviewer-lane dispatch by calling `gsd_run review-lane dispatch-step --cap-id <capId> --point\n<point> --explicit <slugs> ...` — `dispatch-step` resolves its OWN active hook for `<point>` (via\n`resolveActiveHooksForPoint`, the same in-process resolver `loop render-hooks` itself calls) and\nchecks whether `<capId>`'s hook carries this field before proceeding; the workflow does not\nresolve or gate on the trait itself, only passes the two flags naming which step it is. When the\ntrait reads exactly `true`, `dispatch-step` routes through `dispatchReviewerLanes`, the one\ninterpreter in `src/reviewer-step-dispatch.cts` that reuses the existing reviewer-lane selection,\nplanning, and invocation machinery, so any explicitly selected reviewer lane also reviews the\nsame scope. Absent or `false` is inert: `dispatch-step` itself is a no-op (a non-boolean value is\nrejected by `capability-validator.cjs` at load time, so it never reaches `dispatch-step` at all).\nThis is the only place a step opts into reviewer-lane support: a capability beyond `code-review`\nreuses it by declaring the same trait on its own step and calling `dispatch-step` with\n`--cap-id`/`--point`, with zero bespoke TRAIT-RESOLUTION code of its own. The workflow still owns\nmatching its own CLI flags against the reviewer-lane roster and assembling the evidence block\nhanded to its consolidator — those are NOT part of what this trait makes reusable.\n\nA `step` is **advisory by construction**: it never blocks or redirects the host workflow —\nthat is what a `gate` is for. Each dispatch is best-effort; on error record a warning and\ncontinue, honoring `onError`.\n\n**A point whose workflow hand-rolls one `kind` does not implement this contract.** Several\nhost workflows historically matched a single hook (e.g. `execute:post` matched only\n`ref.skill == \"code-review\"`), so any other step registered there was declared and silently\nnever run. When a workflow defers to this file, it dispatches **every** active `step` entry,\nnot one shape of one.\n\n### `gate`\n\n**Validate `check` before any shell use.** `check.query` and `check.predicate` come from a\ncapability manifest, which may be third-party — and `gates[].check` is **not** one of the\nexecutable surfaces the install consent prompt discloses (`hooks`, command modules,\n`mcpServers`, reviewer lanes), so a capability can be consented to as declarative-only and\nstill reach a shell through a gate. Normalize first, then validate: the registry declares query\nids in dotted form (`ui.plan-gate`, `verify.codebase-drift`) and `gsd_run check` accepts them by\nturning each `.` into `-`, so replace every `.` with `-` in the query value you read from\n`activeHooks`, then check that normalized value against\n`^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*( [a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*)*$` yourself, IN-CONTEXT — **never** by\npasting it into a shell command to be tested there, because a value carrying a quote, `;`, a\n`` ` ``, `$(`, or a newline would terminate the assignment and run as its own statement before\nany shell-side check could execute. The normalized value is the one value you validate and the\none value you pass: wherever a workflow shows `${hook.check.query}`, write the normalized value.\nA leading dot, a trailing dot or consecutive dots become a leading, trailing or doubled hyphen,\nwhich the pattern rejects. A value that fails — or that passes but names no known check, which\n`gsd_run check` rejects as an unknown check subcommand — is a malformed manifest: record a\nwarning, route it per the gate's `onError`, and do not run it. Pass `check.predicate` as a **single argv\nelement** for the same reason — never re-quote it into a shell string, where an apostrophe\nwould close the literal. This is the identical requirement `step` → `ref.command` carries\nabove; it was stated there and omitted here, which is the gap #3559 closed.\n\nEvaluate `check` (one of `query`, `predicate`, or `agentVerdict`). Then honor `blocking`:\n\n- `blocking: true` → if the check returns `block: true`, surface `check.message` to the user\n  and stop the current step. Do not continue.\n- `blocking: false` → advisory only; surface the message but continue regardless of outcome.\n\nHonor `onError` if the check itself errors: `skip` means treat as non-blocking and continue;\n`halt` means surface the error and stop. \"Errors\" means a non-zero exit from `gsd_run check ...`,\nand that includes `69` (`UNAVAILABLE`, #5170): a gate verb exits `69` when it **could not look** at\nits evidence, still printing the gate's usual JSON (its own fields name what could not be read, such\nas `status: \"unresolvable\"`, `readError`, or `unreadable`). That JSON is not a verdict — do not act\non its `block`; route it by `onError` as a step-1 command failure. Exit `0` always means a\ndelivered verdict, a blocking one included (`block: true`): a gate verb never exits non-zero to say\n\"blocked\".\n\n## Empty / absent `activeHooks`\n\nIf `activeHooks` is absent, null, or an empty array, skip silently and continue to the next\nstep in the workflow. No output to the user is needed.\n\n## The `execute:task` point (a different shape)\n\n`execute:task` exists below wave granularity — it is evaluated once per task, inside the\n`execute:wave:pre` / `execute:wave:post` bracket, immediately before that task's `read_first`\ngate. It is **not** one of the 12 points documented above, does not appear in `steps` /\n`contributions` / `gates`, and is never dispatched through `gsd_run loop render-hooks <point>` or\nthis file's `activeHooks` envelope.\n\nInstead, a capability declares task-content resolution directly in its manifest body via\n`taskContentResolver` (`trackerPrefix` + a bounded `invoke`) — see\n[Capability manifest reference](../../docs/reference/capability-manifest.md). `execute-plan.md`'s\nper-task loop calls `gsd_run task resolve-content --plan <path> --task-id <tracker-id> --raw`\ndirectly, an unconditional, required subprocess invocation with a real, binding exit code —\nnever a prose-dispatched `step`/`gate` entry chosen from an `activeHooks` array.\n\nThis point always runs — there is no `when` config gate and no autonomous-mode elision. That is\ndeliberate, not an oversight: the twelve points above are best-effort prose dispatch, which\n`execute:task`'s hard-halt safety property cannot be built on top of (a missed dispatch is\nindistinguishable from a legitimate resolver-empty fallback). See\n[ADR-3646](../../docs/adr/3646-per-task-content-resolution-seam.md) for the full rationale,\nincluding why a `kind: \"gate\"` shape was rejected outright.\n","numLines":152,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DZ1Vx2GohNpBwMMuKJn6Bs","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
