{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HWnStJ7oMQMZmNt4K6WDk5","type":"tool_result","content":"119\t<step name=\"compute_file_scope\">\n120\tThree-tier scoping with explicit precedence:\n121\t\n122\tCompute the phase's last review commit, if any. This narrows Tiers 2 and 3 below to what\n123\tchanged since that review (wave-scoped reviews under `workflow.code_review_point=execute:wave:post`):\n124\t\n125\t```bash\n126\t# #3661: incremental scoping — when this phase has a prior review, later tiers\n127\t# narrow to what changed since it (wave-scoped reviews under\n128\t# workflow.code_review_point=execute:wave:post). Empty on a phase's first review\n129\t# (the entire execute:post-default path), in which case Tiers 2 and 3 below are\n130\t# unchanged from today.\n131\tLAST_REVIEW_COMMIT=$(git log --format=%H -1 -- \"${PHASE_DIR}/${PADDED_PHASE}-REVIEW.md\" 2>/dev/null)\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\t**Tier 1 — --files override (highest precedence per D-08):**\n135\t\n136\tIf FILES_OVERRIDE is set (from --files flag):\n137\t```bash\n138\tif [ -n \"$FILES_OVERRIDE\" ]; then\n139\t  REVIEW_FILES=()\n140\t  # #5519: compare the repository root and each resolved path in ONE form. On Windows Git Bash,\n141\t  # `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (a native program) reports a mixed path (C:/Users/..) while\n142\t  # GNU `realpath` reports an MSYS POSIX path (/c/Users/..); the two never share a prefix, so\n143\t  # every entry looked \"outside\". `cygpath -m` exists exactly where the forms differ and maps both\n144\t  # to the mixed form; elsewhere the value passes through unchanged.\n145\t  _gsd_norm_path() {\n146\t    if command -v cygpath >/dev/null 2>&1; then cygpath -m \"$1\"; else printf '%s' \"$1\"; fi\n147\t  }\n148\t  REPO_ROOT=$(_gsd_norm_path \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\")\n149\t  \n150\t  for file_path in \"${FILES_ARRAY[@]}\"; do\n151\t    # Security: validate path is within repository (prevent path traversal). Compare on a path\n152\t    # boundary: the root itself or anything under \"<root>/\" — a sibling such as \"<root>-other\"\n153\t    # shares the prefix but is not inside.\n154\t    ABS_PATH=$(_gsd_norm_path \"$(realpath -m \"${file_path}\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"${file_path}\")\")\n155\t    # Fail closed on two shapes that cannot be trusted: an empty root (git failed, so nothing can be\n156\t    # \"inside\" it) and a path that still holds \"..\" segments (a resolved path never does, so it was\n157\t    # not resolved — no GNU realpath).\n158\t    if [ -z \"$REPO_ROOT\" ] || [[ \"$ABS_PATH\" == */../* || \"$ABS_PATH\" == */.. ]] || [[ \"$ABS_PATH\" != \"$REPO_ROOT\" && \"$ABS_PATH\" != \"$REPO_ROOT\"/* ]]; then\n159\t      echo \"Error: File path outside repository, skipping: ${file_path}\"\n160\t      continue\n161\t    fi\n162\t    \n163\t    # Validate path exists (relative to repo root)\n164\t    if [ -f \"${REPO_ROOT}/${file_path}\" ] || [ -f \"${file_path}\" ]; then\n165\t      REVIEW_FILES+=(\"$file_path\")\n166\t    else\n167\t      echo \"Warning: File not found, skipping: ${file_path}\"\n168\t    fi\n169\t  done\n170\t  \n171\t  echo \"File scope: ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} files from --files override\"\n172\tfi\n173\t```\n174\t\n175\tSkip SUMMARY/git scoping entirely when --files is provided.\n176\t\n177\t**Tier 2 — SUMMARY.md extraction (primary per D-01):**\n178\t\n179\tIf --files NOT provided:\n180\t```bash\n181\tif [ -z \"$FILES_OVERRIDE\" ]; then\n182\t  SUMMARIES=$(ls \"${PHASE_DIR}\"/*-SUMMARY.md 2>/dev/null)\n183\t  REVIEW_FILES=()\n184\t\n185\t  # Keep the literal heredoc outside command substitution: Bash 3.2 (the\n186\t  # system Bash on macOS) reparses heredoc bodies nested directly in $(...).\n187\t  extract_summary_files() {\n188\t    node - \"$1\" 2>/dev/null <<'NODE'\n189\t    const fs = require('fs');\n190\t    const content = fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf-8');\n191\t    const match = content.replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n').match(/^---\\n([\\s\\S]*?)\\n---/);\n192\t    if (!match) { process.exit(0); }\n193\t    const yaml = match[1];\n194\t    const files = [];\n195\t    let inSection = null;\n196\t    for (const line of yaml.split('\\n')) {\n197\t      if (/^\\s+created:/.test(line)) { inSection = 'created'; continue; }\n198\t      if (/^\\s+modified:/.test(line)) { inSection = 'modified'; continue; }\n199\t      if (/^\\s*[\\w-]+:/.test(line) && !/^\\s*-/.test(line)) { inSection = null; continue; }\n200\t      if (inSection && /^\\s+-\\s+(.+)/.test(line)) {\n201\t        let raw = line.match(/^\\s+-\\s+(.+)/)[1].trim();\n202\t        raw = raw.replace(/^['\"]|['\"]$/g, '');\n203\t        raw = raw.replace(/\\s+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*$/, '');\n204\t        raw = raw.split(/\\s+—\\s/)[0].trim();\n205\t        // #2666: accept root-level paths (no `/`) and known extensionless build\n206\t        // files, not only nested paths with a trailing extension. The pre-fix\n207\t        // guard required BOTH a directory separator AND a trailing dot-extension,\n208\t        // which silently dropped every repository-root file (Dockerfile,\n209\t        // renovate.json, AGENTS.md, package.json, .gitlab-ci.yml, …) and every\n210\t        // extensionless build file anywhere in the tree (**/Dockerfile, **/Makefile).\n211\t        // Prose bullets are rejected by the known-filename / has-extension\n212\t        // distinction, with the post-processing existence check (`[ -f ]`) as a\n213\t        // backstop — a prose string is never a real file on disk.\n214\t        const KNOWN_EXTENSIONLESS_BUILD_FILES = new Set([\n215\t          'dockerfile', 'containerfile', 'makefile', 'justfile', 'procfile',\n216\t        ]);\n217\t        const hasExtension = /\\.[A-Za-z0-9]+$/.test(raw);\n218\t        const basename = raw.split('/').pop().toLowerCase();\n219\t        if (hasExtension || KNOWN_EXTENSIONLESS_BUILD_FILES.has(basename)) {\n220\t          files.push(raw);\n221\t        }\n222\t      }\n223\t    }\n224\t    if (files.length) console.log(files.join('\\n'));\n225\tNODE\n226\t  }\n227\t  \n228\t  if [ -n \"$SUMMARIES\" ]; then\n229\t    # Rewrapped through unquoted command substitution (gsd-core#4109): a bare\n230\t    # `$VAR` word-splits under bash but not zsh, collapsing every element onto\n231\t    # one iteration there.\n232\t    for summary in $(printf '%s' \"$SUMMARIES\"); do\n233\t      # #3661: skip a SUMMARY.md unchanged since the phase's last review — this\n234\t      # summary's plan was already reviewed. No-op (every summary is \"changed\") when\n235\t      # LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT is empty. Fails OPEN on any git error (file stays in scope)\n236\t      # — never silently drop a file because a git command errored.\n237\t      if [ -n \"$LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT\" ] && git diff --quiet \"${LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT}\" HEAD -- \"$summary\" 2>/dev/null; then\n238\t        continue\n239\t      fi\n240\t\n241\t      # Extract key_files.created and key_files.modified using node for reliable YAML parsing\n242\t      # This avoids fragile awk parsing that breaks on indentation differences\n243\t      EXTRACTED=$(extract_summary_files \"$summary\")\n244\t      \n245\t      # Add extracted files to REVIEW_FILES array\n246\t      if [ -n \"$EXTRACTED\" ]; then\n247\t        while IFS= read -r file; do\n248\t          if [ -n \"$file\" ]; then\n249\t            REVIEW_FILES+=(\"$file\")\n250\t          fi\n251\t        done <<< \"$EXTRACTED\"\n252\t      fi\n253\t    done\n254\t    \n255\t    if [ ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n256\t      echo \"Warning: SUMMARY artifacts found but contained no file paths. Falling back to the phase evaluation scope.\"\n257\t    fi\n258\t  fi\n259\tfi\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\t**Tier 3 — Phase evaluation scope (per D-02, #5164) and SUMMARY/scope cross-check (per #2666):**\n263\t\n264\tIf no SUMMARY.md files found OR no files extracted from them, fall back to the phase's evaluation scope.\n265\tAdditionally, whenever the resolver returns a scope, cross-check the SUMMARY scope against it and warn\n266\tabout (then add) any in-scope files the SUMMARY extractor did not surface — so a partial SUMMARY result\n267\tcan no longer silently mask the rest of the phase.\n268\t\n269\tThe scope is computed by the ONE resolver, `check evaluation-scope` (ADR-5057 §4, #5164) — this step\n270\tderives no commit range of its own. The resolver returns the UNION of the phase's own commits' file\n271\tsets (the `## Task Commits` rows of the phase's SUMMARYs), restricted to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n272\t(a commit that lives only on another branch is named, never counted), with planning artifacts and\n273\tlockfiles excluded. A range (`base..HEAD`) is deliberately not used: it keeps every interleaved\n274\tnon-phase commit in its window (#3926, #4563). The resolver also reports, by name rather than by\n275\tcount, what the union dropped: `outsideUnion` (changed in the phase window by other commits) and\n276\t`unreachable` (task commits not on this branch). When the union is empty — a phase with no SUMMARY,\n277\tno task rows, or only planning paths — the resolver widens to the phase-directory range and says so\n278\t(`status: degraded`, `reason`); an unreadable phase or repository is `unresolvable`, never an empty\n279\tscope that reports success. Its `rangeBase` (the parent of the commit that first added the phase\n280\tdirectory, or `LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT` when a prior review exists — #3661) is `DIFF_BASE`, kept only for\n281\tthe reviewer lanes that need an anchor sha.\n282\t```bash\n283\t# Exit 69 (UNAVAILABLE) is the resolver saying \"could not look\" (`status: unresolvable`): its JSON\n284\t# carries the reason and is kept. Any other non-zero status means the command did not run: no JSON.\n285\tSCOPE_RC=0\n286\tSCOPE_JSON=$(gsd_run check evaluation-scope --phase \"${PADDED_PHASE}\" ${LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT:+--since \"$LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT\"} --raw 2>/dev/null) || SCOPE_RC=$?\n287\tif [ \"$SCOPE_RC\" -ne 0 ] && [ \"$SCOPE_RC\" -ne 69 ]; then SCOPE_JSON=\"\"; fi\n288\t# One field of the resolver's JSON; arrays print one element per line, an absent field prints nothing.\n289\tscope_field() {\n290\t  printf '%s' \"$SCOPE_JSON\" | node -e 'let s=\"\";process.stdin.on(\"data\",d=>s+=d).on(\"end\",()=>{try{const v=JSON.parse(s)[process.argv[1]];process.stdout.write(Array.isArray(v)?v.join(\"\\n\"):v==null?\"\":String(v))}catch{process.exit(1)}})' \"$1\" 2>/dev/null\n291\t}\n292\tSCOPE_STATUS=$(scope_field status); [ -n \"$SCOPE_STATUS\" ] || SCOPE_STATUS=\"unresolvable\"\n293\tSCOPE_REASON=$(scope_field reason)\n294\tSCOPE_SOURCE=$(scope_field source)\n295\tDIFF_BASE=$(scope_field rangeBase)\n296\tSCOPE_FILES=$(scope_field files)\n297\t\n298\tif [ \"$SCOPE_STATUS\" != \"resolved\" ]; then\n299\t  echo \"Warning: evaluation scope is ${SCOPE_STATUS}${SCOPE_REASON:+ (${SCOPE_REASON})}; the file scope below comes from wider evidence or is unavailable.\"\n300\tfi\n301\tSCOPE_OUTSIDE=$(scope_field outsideUnion)\n302\tif [ -n \"$SCOPE_OUTSIDE\" ]; then\n303\t  echo \"Changed in the phase window by commits that are NOT this phase's task commits (not in scope):\"\n304\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_OUTSIDE\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\n305\tfi\n306\tSCOPE_UNREACHABLE=$(scope_field unreachable)\n307\tif [ -n \"$SCOPE_UNREACHABLE\" ]; then\n308\t  echo \"Task commits not reachable from HEAD (another branch, or dropped by a rebase):\"\n309\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_UNREACHABLE\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\n310\tfi\n311\tSCOPE_MISSING=$(scope_field missingOnDisk)\n312\tif [ -n \"$SCOPE_MISSING\" ]; then\n313\t  echo \"Scoped paths that no longer exist on disk (not reviewed):\"\n314\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_MISSING\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\n315\tfi\n316\t\n317\tif [ ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n318\t  # Full fallback (per D-02): SUMMARY scoping yielded nothing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"uuid-7","uuid":"uuid-405","timestamp":"2026-11-02T18:55:29.068Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"~/.claude/gsd-core/workflows/code-review.md","content":"<step name=\"compute_file_scope\">\nThree-tier scoping with explicit precedence:\n\nCompute the phase's last review commit, if any. This narrows Tiers 2 and 3 below to what\nchanged since that review (wave-scoped reviews under `workflow.code_review_point=execute:wave:post`):\n\n```bash\n# #3661: incremental scoping — when this phase has a prior review, later tiers\n# narrow to what changed since it (wave-scoped reviews under\n# workflow.code_review_point=execute:wave:post). Empty on a phase's first review\n# (the entire execute:post-default path), in which case Tiers 2 and 3 below are\n# unchanged from today.\nLAST_REVIEW_COMMIT=$(git log --format=%H -1 -- \"${PHASE_DIR}/${PADDED_PHASE}-REVIEW.md\" 2>/dev/null)\n```\n\n**Tier 1 — --files override (highest precedence per D-08):**\n\nIf FILES_OVERRIDE is set (from --files flag):\n```bash\nif [ -n \"$FILES_OVERRIDE\" ]; then\n  REVIEW_FILES=()\n  # #5519: compare the repository root and each resolved path in ONE form. On Windows Git Bash,\n  # `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (a native program) reports a mixed path (C:/Users/..) while\n  # GNU `realpath` reports an MSYS POSIX path (/c/Users/..); the two never share a prefix, so\n  # every entry looked \"outside\". `cygpath -m` exists exactly where the forms differ and maps both\n  # to the mixed form; elsewhere the value passes through unchanged.\n  _gsd_norm_path() {\n    if command -v cygpath >/dev/null 2>&1; then cygpath -m \"$1\"; else printf '%s' \"$1\"; fi\n  }\n  REPO_ROOT=$(_gsd_norm_path \"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\")\n  \n  for file_path in \"${FILES_ARRAY[@]}\"; do\n    # Security: validate path is within repository (prevent path traversal). Compare on a path\n    # boundary: the root itself or anything under \"<root>/\" — a sibling such as \"<root>-other\"\n    # shares the prefix but is not inside.\n    ABS_PATH=$(_gsd_norm_path \"$(realpath -m \"${file_path}\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"${file_path}\")\")\n    # Fail closed on two shapes that cannot be trusted: an empty root (git failed, so nothing can be\n    # \"inside\" it) and a path that still holds \"..\" segments (a resolved path never does, so it was\n    # not resolved — no GNU realpath).\n    if [ -z \"$REPO_ROOT\" ] || [[ \"$ABS_PATH\" == */../* || \"$ABS_PATH\" == */.. ]] || [[ \"$ABS_PATH\" != \"$REPO_ROOT\" && \"$ABS_PATH\" != \"$REPO_ROOT\"/* ]]; then\n      echo \"Error: File path outside repository, skipping: ${file_path}\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    \n    # Validate path exists (relative to repo root)\n    if [ -f \"${REPO_ROOT}/${file_path}\" ] || [ -f \"${file_path}\" ]; then\n      REVIEW_FILES+=(\"$file_path\")\n    else\n      echo \"Warning: File not found, skipping: ${file_path}\"\n    fi\n  done\n  \n  echo \"File scope: ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} files from --files override\"\nfi\n```\n\nSkip SUMMARY/git scoping entirely when --files is provided.\n\n**Tier 2 — SUMMARY.md extraction (primary per D-01):**\n\nIf --files NOT provided:\n```bash\nif [ -z \"$FILES_OVERRIDE\" ]; then\n  SUMMARIES=$(ls \"${PHASE_DIR}\"/*-SUMMARY.md 2>/dev/null)\n  REVIEW_FILES=()\n\n  # Keep the literal heredoc outside command substitution: Bash 3.2 (the\n  # system Bash on macOS) reparses heredoc bodies nested directly in $(...).\n  extract_summary_files() {\n    node - \"$1\" 2>/dev/null <<'NODE'\n    const fs = require('fs');\n    const content = fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf-8');\n    const match = content.replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n').match(/^---\\n([\\s\\S]*?)\\n---/);\n    if (!match) { process.exit(0); }\n    const yaml = match[1];\n    const files = [];\n    let inSection = null;\n    for (const line of yaml.split('\\n')) {\n      if (/^\\s+created:/.test(line)) { inSection = 'created'; continue; }\n      if (/^\\s+modified:/.test(line)) { inSection = 'modified'; continue; }\n      if (/^\\s*[\\w-]+:/.test(line) && !/^\\s*-/.test(line)) { inSection = null; continue; }\n      if (inSection && /^\\s+-\\s+(.+)/.test(line)) {\n        let raw = line.match(/^\\s+-\\s+(.+)/)[1].trim();\n        raw = raw.replace(/^['\"]|['\"]$/g, '');\n        raw = raw.replace(/\\s+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*$/, '');\n        raw = raw.split(/\\s+—\\s/)[0].trim();\n        // #2666: accept root-level paths (no `/`) and known extensionless build\n        // files, not only nested paths with a trailing extension. The pre-fix\n        // guard required BOTH a directory separator AND a trailing dot-extension,\n        // which silently dropped every repository-root file (Dockerfile,\n        // renovate.json, AGENTS.md, package.json, .gitlab-ci.yml, …) and every\n        // extensionless build file anywhere in the tree (**/Dockerfile, **/Makefile).\n        // Prose bullets are rejected by the known-filename / has-extension\n        // distinction, with the post-processing existence check (`[ -f ]`) as a\n        // backstop — a prose string is never a real file on disk.\n        const KNOWN_EXTENSIONLESS_BUILD_FILES = new Set([\n          'dockerfile', 'containerfile', 'makefile', 'justfile', 'procfile',\n        ]);\n        const hasExtension = /\\.[A-Za-z0-9]+$/.test(raw);\n        const basename = raw.split('/').pop().toLowerCase();\n        if (hasExtension || KNOWN_EXTENSIONLESS_BUILD_FILES.has(basename)) {\n          files.push(raw);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if (files.length) console.log(files.join('\\n'));\nNODE\n  }\n  \n  if [ -n \"$SUMMARIES\" ]; then\n    # Rewrapped through unquoted command substitution (gsd-core#4109): a bare\n    # `$VAR` word-splits under bash but not zsh, collapsing every element onto\n    # one iteration there.\n    for summary in $(printf '%s' \"$SUMMARIES\"); do\n      # #3661: skip a SUMMARY.md unchanged since the phase's last review — this\n      # summary's plan was already reviewed. No-op (every summary is \"changed\") when\n      # LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT is empty. Fails OPEN on any git error (file stays in scope)\n      # — never silently drop a file because a git command errored.\n      if [ -n \"$LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT\" ] && git diff --quiet \"${LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT}\" HEAD -- \"$summary\" 2>/dev/null; then\n        continue\n      fi\n\n      # Extract key_files.created and key_files.modified using node for reliable YAML parsing\n      # This avoids fragile awk parsing that breaks on indentation differences\n      EXTRACTED=$(extract_summary_files \"$summary\")\n      \n      # Add extracted files to REVIEW_FILES array\n      if [ -n \"$EXTRACTED\" ]; then\n        while IFS= read -r file; do\n          if [ -n \"$file\" ]; then\n            REVIEW_FILES+=(\"$file\")\n          fi\n        done <<< \"$EXTRACTED\"\n      fi\n    done\n    \n    if [ ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n      echo \"Warning: SUMMARY artifacts found but contained no file paths. Falling back to the phase evaluation scope.\"\n    fi\n  fi\nfi\n```\n\n**Tier 3 — Phase evaluation scope (per D-02, #5164) and SUMMARY/scope cross-check (per #2666):**\n\nIf no SUMMARY.md files found OR no files extracted from them, fall back to the phase's evaluation scope.\nAdditionally, whenever the resolver returns a scope, cross-check the SUMMARY scope against it and warn\nabout (then add) any in-scope files the SUMMARY extractor did not surface — so a partial SUMMARY result\ncan no longer silently mask the rest of the phase.\n\nThe scope is computed by the ONE resolver, `check evaluation-scope` (ADR-5057 §4, #5164) — this step\nderives no commit range of its own. The resolver returns the UNION of the phase's own commits' file\nsets (the `## Task Commits` rows of the phase's SUMMARYs), restricted to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n(a commit that lives only on another branch is named, never counted), with planning artifacts and\nlockfiles excluded. A range (`base..HEAD`) is deliberately not used: it keeps every interleaved\nnon-phase commit in its window (#3926, #4563). The resolver also reports, by name rather than by\ncount, what the union dropped: `outsideUnion` (changed in the phase window by other commits) and\n`unreachable` (task commits not on this branch). When the union is empty — a phase with no SUMMARY,\nno task rows, or only planning paths — the resolver widens to the phase-directory range and says so\n(`status: degraded`, `reason`); an unreadable phase or repository is `unresolvable`, never an empty\nscope that reports success. Its `rangeBase` (the parent of the commit that first added the phase\ndirectory, or `LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT` when a prior review exists — #3661) is `DIFF_BASE`, kept only for\nthe reviewer lanes that need an anchor sha.\n```bash\n# Exit 69 (UNAVAILABLE) is the resolver saying \"could not look\" (`status: unresolvable`): its JSON\n# carries the reason and is kept. Any other non-zero status means the command did not run: no JSON.\nSCOPE_RC=0\nSCOPE_JSON=$(gsd_run check evaluation-scope --phase \"${PADDED_PHASE}\" ${LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT:+--since \"$LAST_REVIEW_COMMIT\"} --raw 2>/dev/null) || SCOPE_RC=$?\nif [ \"$SCOPE_RC\" -ne 0 ] && [ \"$SCOPE_RC\" -ne 69 ]; then SCOPE_JSON=\"\"; fi\n# One field of the resolver's JSON; arrays print one element per line, an absent field prints nothing.\nscope_field() {\n  printf '%s' \"$SCOPE_JSON\" | node -e 'let s=\"\";process.stdin.on(\"data\",d=>s+=d).on(\"end\",()=>{try{const v=JSON.parse(s)[process.argv[1]];process.stdout.write(Array.isArray(v)?v.join(\"\\n\"):v==null?\"\":String(v))}catch{process.exit(1)}})' \"$1\" 2>/dev/null\n}\nSCOPE_STATUS=$(scope_field status); [ -n \"$SCOPE_STATUS\" ] || SCOPE_STATUS=\"unresolvable\"\nSCOPE_REASON=$(scope_field reason)\nSCOPE_SOURCE=$(scope_field source)\nDIFF_BASE=$(scope_field rangeBase)\nSCOPE_FILES=$(scope_field files)\n\nif [ \"$SCOPE_STATUS\" != \"resolved\" ]; then\n  echo \"Warning: evaluation scope is ${SCOPE_STATUS}${SCOPE_REASON:+ (${SCOPE_REASON})}; the file scope below comes from wider evidence or is unavailable.\"\nfi\nSCOPE_OUTSIDE=$(scope_field outsideUnion)\nif [ -n \"$SCOPE_OUTSIDE\" ]; then\n  echo \"Changed in the phase window by commits that are NOT this phase's task commits (not in scope):\"\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_OUTSIDE\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\nfi\nSCOPE_UNREACHABLE=$(scope_field unreachable)\nif [ -n \"$SCOPE_UNREACHABLE\" ]; then\n  echo \"Task commits not reachable from HEAD (another branch, or dropped by a rebase):\"\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_UNREACHABLE\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\nfi\nSCOPE_MISSING=$(scope_field missingOnDisk)\nif [ -n \"$SCOPE_MISSING\" ]; then\n  echo \"Scoped paths that no longer exist on disk (not reviewed):\"\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$SCOPE_MISSING\" | sed 's/^/  - /'\nfi\n\nif [ ${#REVIEW_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  # Full fallback (per D-02): SUMMARY scoping yielded nothing.","numLines":200,"startLine":119,"totalLines":1021}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HWnStJ7oMQMZmNt4K6WDk5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
